<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonicalUrl} lang="en" hreflang={hreflang} schema={articleSchema}>
  <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">Statistics &amp; Data · UAE 2026</p>
      <h1>Divorce Rate in UAE and Dubai 2026: Statistics, Trends &amp; Data by Emirate</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-sub">The latest official figures are for 2024: Dubai Courts registered 2,285 divorce cases, and the divorce rate for Emirati couples in Dubai was 1.96 per 1,000 Emiratis of marriage age (Dubai Statistics Center). For the rest of the UAE, official figures come emirate by emirate: Ministry of Justice data put 2024 divorces at the federal courts of Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain at 448 to 473, and Ras Al Khaimah courts registered 485 divorces in 2023.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;margin-top:24px;">
        <a href="#contact" style="display:inline-block;padding:13px 28px;background:#c9a84c;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Talk to a Divorce Lawyer</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/971557344876?text=Hello%2C%20I%20need%20divorce%20legal%20advice%20in%20UAE" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:13px 28px;background:#25d366;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="main-content">
    <figure style="margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/images/divorce-rate-uae.webp" alt="UAE divorce rate: the Dubai and Abu Dhabi skyline at golden hour" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;" />
    </figure>
    <div class="container">

      <!-- Stats cards -->
      <div class="stats-row">
        {statsCards.map(s => (
          <div class="stat-card">
            <p class="stat-number">{s.stat}</p>
            <p class="stat-label">{s.label}</p>
            <p class="stat-note">{s.note}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <!-- Context -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Understanding UAE Divorce Statistics</h2>
        <p>UAE divorce data comes from several separate bodies, and they do not measure the same thing. The Federal Competitiveness and Statistics Centre (FCSC) publishes registered marriage contracts and divorce certificates by emirate, nationality and gender on its data portals. The Dubai Statistics Center publishes divorce and marriage rates for Emiratis built from Dubai Courts records, together with case counts for all nationalities. The Ministry of Justice releases counts from the federal courts that serve Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain. Ras Al Khaimah and Abu Dhabi have their own courts and statistics centres. Because each source counts a different population, quoting a single "UAE divorce rate" without saying whose rate it is can be misleading.</p>
        <p>There is a second problem: rates are published for Emiratis, while most residents are expatriates. Dubai, for example, had about 295,500 Emirati residents in mid-2024 according to the Dubai Statistics Center, a minority of the emirate’s population. Many expatriates marry or divorce in their home countries, so UAE counts are not a measure of how often expatriate marriages end.</p>
        <p>The Dubai Statistics Center uses three standard measures, and they produce very different-looking numbers from the same cases: the <strong>crude divorce rate</strong> (divorces per 1,000 people in the group), the <strong>general divorce rate</strong> (per 1,000 people of marriage age, 15 and over), and the <strong>net or refined divorce rate</strong> (per 1,000 married people). For Emirati couples in Dubai in 2024 those were 1.32, 1.96 and 4.16. Check which one a headline is using before drawing conclusions. If you are weighing your own situation, our <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">complete guide to divorce in the UAE</a> explains the process behind the numbers.</p>

        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>What a Divorce-to-Marriage Ratio Actually Means</h3>
          <p>A divorce-to-marriage ratio of, say, 26% does NOT mean 26% of marriages end in divorce. It means that for every 100 marriages registered in a year, 26 divorces were also registered, but those divorces come from marriages that began in earlier years. During periods of falling marriage numbers, the ratio can look alarming even when the underlying divorce rate is stable or dropping. Always check whether a figure is a per-1,000 rate, an annual count, or a ratio.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Headline data -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Latest Official Numbers</h2>
        <p>Here are the most recent figures we could verify, each tied to its source and year. Where a number reaches the public through a news report of official data rather than the statistics office itself, the report is named.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th>Figure</th>
                <th>Source &amp; year</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-label">Divorce cases registered in Dubai</td>
                <td class="td-cost">2,285</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2024, Dubai Courts data in the Dubai Statistics Center report "Divorce and Marriage Rates for Emiratis, Emirate of Dubai 2022 to 2024". 2,017 in 2023.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-alt">
                <td class="td-label">Marriage contracts registered in Dubai</td>
                <td class="td-cost">6,864</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2024, same report. 6,311 in 2023.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-label">Dubai Emirati general divorce rate</td>
                <td class="td-cost">1.96 / 1,000</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2024, both spouses Emirati, per 1,000 Emiratis aged 15 and over living in Dubai (Dubai Statistics Center).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-alt">
                <td class="td-label">Dubai Emirati crude divorce rate, all Emirati cases</td>
                <td class="td-cost">2.17 / 1,000</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2024, per 1,000 Emirati residents of Dubai, including marriages with one Emirati spouse. 3.76 in 2016 (Dubai Statistics Center).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-label">Divorces at the four northern federal courts</td>
                <td class="td-cost">448 to 473</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2024, Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain, Ministry of Justice data as reported by Gulf News (March 2025: 448) and Khaleej Times (December 2025: 473).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-alt">
                <td class="td-label">Same courts, latest reported year</td>
                <td class="td-cost">519</td>
                <td class="td-notes">Ministry of Justice data reported by Gulf News (updated 1 February 2026); the report refers to "last year" without naming it.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-label">Divorces within the first year of marriage</td>
                <td class="td-cost">~30%</td>
                <td class="td-notes">851 of 2,857 cases at the four northern federal courts, 2020 to 2024 (Ministry of Justice, reported by Khaleej Times, December 2025).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-alt">
                <td class="td-label">Divorce cases registered in Ras Al Khaimah</td>
                <td class="td-cost">485</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2023, RAK Courts Department data in the RAK Statistics Centre Statistical Yearbook 2024. 473 in 2022.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-label">Divorces granted in Abu Dhabi</td>
                <td class="td-cost">2,086</td>
                <td class="td-notes">2019, Abu Dhabi Judicial Department data in the Statistical Yearbook of Abu Dhabi 2020 (Statistics Centre Abu Dhabi), the latest emirate-wide figure we could verify.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The most striking pattern in this data is timing, not volume. The Ministry of Justice release reported by Gulf News in February 2026 covered 519 divorces at the northern courts and included 14 marriages that lasted less than a month, one ending within hours of the contract and another after a single day, alongside separations after 36, 40 and even 58 years of marriage. Marriages in the UAE end across the entire spectrum of duration, but the concentration in the first year is what draws the most concern from courts and counsellors.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Dubai -->
      <section class="content-section" id="dubai">
        <h2>Divorce Rate in Dubai: The Official 2024 Figures</h2>
        <p>Dubai Courts registered <strong>2,285 divorce cases in 2024</strong>, up 13% from 2,017 in 2023, and the general divorce rate for Emirati couples was <strong>1.96 per 1,000 Emiratis of marriage age</strong>, almost unchanged from 1.97 in 2023 and 1.90 in 2022. Dubai is the one emirate with a long official series of divorce rates, published by the Dubai Statistics Center from Dubai Courts records, and it tells two different stories depending on whose divorces you count.</p>
        <p>For Emiratis, the long-run trend is down. The crude divorce rate for all divorces involving an Emirati fell 35% between 2016 and 2019, from 3.76 to 2.44 per 1,000 Emirati residents, and the net rate per 1,000 married Emiratis fell about 33.5%, from 11.95 to 7.95. In 2024 those measures stood at 2.17 and 6.84. For the whole caseload, which is mostly expatriate couples, the count is up: 2024 was the highest annual number of divorce cases in the official reports since 2016.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Year</th>
                <th>Divorce cases, all nationalities</th>
                <th>Of which two non-Emiratis</th>
                <th>Emirati crude rate per 1,000</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {dubaiSeries.map((r, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{r.year}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{r.cases}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{r.expat}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{r.crude}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Source: Dubai Statistics Center, "Divorce and Marriage Rates for Emiratis, Emirate of Dubai" reports for 2016 to 2019, 2020 to 2022, 2021 to 2023 and 2022 to 2024 (case counts from Dubai Courts). The crude rate counts every divorce involving at least one Emirati spouse per 1,000 Emirati residents. Where reports overlap, the latest is used; earlier editions give slightly different values for some years.</p>
        <p>Two caveats matter. First, the rates are Emirati-only measures; they say nothing directly about the expatriate majority. Second, a flat or falling per-capita rate can sit alongside a rising number of court cases when the population is growing, which is why headlines sometimes appear to contradict each other. Both can be true at once.</p>

        <div class="trend-list">
          {[
            { year: '2016', trend: 'Baseline', desc: '2,170 divorce cases in Dubai. Emirati crude rate 3.76 per 1,000 Emirati residents; net rate 11.95 per 1,000 married Emiratis (Dubai Statistics Center).' },
            { year: '2019', trend: 'Down 35%', desc: 'Emirati crude rate 2.44 and net rate 7.95, falls of 35% and 33.5% in three years. 1,729 cases, the lowest count in the series.' },
            { year: '2020 to 2021', trend: 'Pandemic years', desc: '1,868 cases in 2020 and 2,106 in 2021; the Emirati crude rate moved from 2.31 to 2.50 (Dubai Statistics Center).' },
            { year: '2023', trend: 'Reform takes effect', desc: 'Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 (civil, non-Muslim) in force from 1 February; 2,017 divorce cases registered in Dubai.' },
            { year: '2024 to 2025', trend: 'Record count, steady Emirati rate', desc: '2,285 cases in 2024, the most since 2016, with the Emirati general rate at 1.96. Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 replaced the 2005 Muslim personal status law from 15 April 2025.' },
          ].map(t => (
            <div class="trend-item">
              <div class="trend-year">{t.year}</div>
              <div class="trend-content">
                <span class="trend-label">{t.trend}</span>
                <p>{t.desc}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Emirate breakdown -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Divorce Data by Emirate</h2>
        <p>Numbers vary across the seven emirates, driven mainly by population size and by which court publishes the data. All figures below are absolute case counts, so a higher figure reflects a larger population, not necessarily a higher per-capita rate. The years differ because each emirate publishes on its own schedule.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Emirate</th>
                <th>Latest figure</th>
                <th>Key context</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {emirateData.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'row-alt'}>
                  <td class="td-label">{row.emirate}</td>
                  <td class="td-cost">{row.divorceRatio}</td>
                  <td class="td-notes">{row.note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>For expatriates, where you divorce shapes both the process and the outcome. Abu Dhabi’s Civil Family Court and the federal civil law offer a no-fault route, while Muslims go through the family courts under the 2024 law. Our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-for-expats-in-uae/">divorce for expats in the UAE</a> and the overview of <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce in the UAE</a> explain how these venues differ in practice.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Emirati vs expat -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Emiratis vs Expatriates: A Two-Track Picture</h2>
        <p>Official statistics describe two different populations, and the mix changes by emirate. Dubai is the clearest case: in 2024, 1,644 of its 2,285 divorce cases (72%) were between two non-Emiratis, 390 were between two Emiratis and 251 involved one Emirati spouse. Non-Emirati couples made up a similar share of marriages, 4,636 of 6,864 contracts (68%).</p>
        <p>Elsewhere the picture flips. In the 2024 Ministry of Justice figures reported by Gulf News for the four northern federal courts, Emirati couples were the largest group, 198 of 448 cases, against 135 expatriate couples, 102 Emirati husbands with non-Emirati wives and 13 Emirati wives with non-Emirati husbands. In Ras Al Khaimah in 2023, 258 of 485 cases were between two citizens and 150 between two non-citizens. For the practical side of what happens next, see our guides on <a href="/en/grounds-for-divorce-uae/">grounds for divorce in the UAE</a> and <a href="/en/remarriage-after-divorce-uae/">remarriage after divorce</a>.</p>
        <p>The expatriate picture may now be shifting because the civil law gives non-Muslims a straightforward UAE route. In Dubai, divorces between two non-Emiratis rose from 1,396 in 2023 to 1,644 in 2024. The official report does not say why, and population growth may also play a part, but as more expatriate divorces are recorded in the UAE, official counts can rise even if the underlying rate of relationship breakdown does not. That is the biggest reason to be careful comparing pre-2023 and post-2023 numbers.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Causes -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Why UAE Marriages End: Cited Causes</h2>
        <p>Family specialists quoted alongside the official figures point to a consistent set of causes. The labels below reflect how often each factor is cited in that reporting, not a measured share of cases; no UAE statistics office publishes divorce causes as a current series.</p>

        <div class="cause-list">
          {causes.map(c => (
            <div class="cause-card">
              <div class="cause-header">
                <span class="cause-name">{c.cause}</span>
                <span class="cause-freq">{c.frequency}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="cause-detail">{c.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Government response -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Programmes That Aim to Settle Disputes Before Divorce</h2>
        <p>The UAE runs several programmes aimed at resolving family disputes before they reach a divorce judgment. They mainly affect Muslim couples, because the civil route for non-Muslims skips reconciliation by design.</p>

        <div class="initiative-list">
          {[
            { initiative: 'Family Guidance before court (Muslim cases)', detail: 'Under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, the supervising judge may refer a couple to Family Guidance if a settlement looks feasible (Article 8). Cases to prove a divorce are exempt. A settlement reached there is signed, approved by the judge and enforceable like a court order.' },
            { initiative: 'Abu Dhabi Family Guidance Department', detail: 'The Abu Dhabi Judicial Department’s Family Guidance Department, founded in 1998, holds sessions by video call and introduced a Family Programme for Divorce in 2020 to help couples settle disputes outside court.' },
            { initiative: 'No reconciliation stage for civil divorces', detail: 'Non-Muslims divorcing under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 are not referred to Family Guidance; the court can rule at the first hearing (Article 3). Couples can still choose mediation voluntarily.' },
            { initiative: 'Official measurement in Dubai', detail: 'Dubai’s Community Development Authority produced the 2016 to 2019 divorce-rate study with the Dubai Statistics Center and Dubai Courts (reported by Gulf News, May 2020), and the Dubai Statistics Center has published updated rate reports since, which is why Dubai has the most complete series in the country.' },
          ].map((item, i) => (
            <div class="tip-item">
              <div class="tip-number">{i + 1}</div>
              <div class="tip-content">
                <h3>{item.initiative}</h3>
                <p>{item.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- What it means -->
      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What These Numbers Mean If You Are Considering Divorce</h2>
        <p>Statistics describe populations, not people. A steady Emirati rate in Dubai or a 30% first-year figure tells you nothing about whether your own marriage should end. What the data can usefully tell you is that you are not an outlier, that early-marriage strain is common and well understood by UAE courts, and that the system now has clearer pathways than it did a few years ago.</p>
        <p>Three practical points follow from the numbers. First, the law you fall under matters more than any rate: Muslims come under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, while non-Muslims can use the civil no-fault route under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022. Our overview of <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law</a> breaks down which applies to you. Second, timing and process differ sharply by track, which our <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">UAE divorce timeline</a> guide sets out step by step. Third, cost varies widely between agreed and contested cases; the <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">divorce cost guide</a> gives the official court fees so the financial picture is not a surprise.</p>
        <p>The strongest signal in the UAE data is that early intervention matters. Specialists quoted alongside the Ministry of Justice figures stress that many first-year divorces stem from preparation gaps and unmet expectations rather than irreparable conflict. If your situation is unresolved rather than final, that is worth weighing before filing.</p>
      </section>

